{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat datasets/derived/secret_words.json | head -80; sed -n '1,130p' src/centered_probe.py; cat results/generation_config.json | head -100; cat results/analysis_replay.json | head -20; cat results/final_validation.json | head -55; .venv/bin/python -c \\\"import importlib.util; print('fitz',bool(importlib.util.find_spec('fitz')))\\\"; sed -n '10,60p' src/analyze.py\"","aggregated_output":"[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"curated_concrete_0\",\n    \"word\": \"umbrella\",\n    \"category\": \"concrete\",\n    \"source\": \"2605.10794 Appendix D\",\n    \"provenance\": \"transcribed from paper; not downloaded author data\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"curated_concrete_1\",\n    \"word\": \"lighthouse\",\n    \"category\": \"concrete\",\n    \"source\": \"2605.10794 Appendix D\",\n    \"provenance\": \"transcribed from paper; not downloaded author data\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"curated_concrete_2\",\n    \"word\": \"violin\",\n    \"category\": \"concrete\",\n    \"source\": \"2605.10794 Appendix D\",\n    \"provenance\": \"transcribed from paper; not downloaded author data\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"curated_concrete_3\",\n    \"word\": \"cactus\",\n    \"category\": \"concrete\",\n    \"source\": \"2605.10794 Appendix D\",\n    \"provenance\": \"transcribed from paper; not downloaded author data\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"curated_concrete_4\",\n    \"word\": \"telescope\",\n    \"category\": \"concrete\",\n    \"source\": \"2605.10794 Appendix D\",\n    \"provenance\": \"transcribed from paper; not downloaded author data\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"curated_abstract_0\",\n    \"word\": \"justice\",\n    \"category\": \"abstract\",\n    \"source\": \"2605.10794 Appendix D\",\n    \"provenance\": \"transcribed from paper; not downloaded author data\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"curated_abstract_1\",\n    \"word\": \"patience\",\n    \"category\": \"abstract\",\n    \"source\": \"2605.10794 Appendix D\",\n    \"provenance\": \"transcribed from paper; not downloaded author data\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"curated_abstract_2\",\n    \"word\": \"entropy\",\n    \"category\": \"abstract\",\n    \"source\": \"2605.10794 Appendix D\",\n    \"provenance\": \"transcribed from paper; not downloaded author data\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"curated_abstract_3\",\n    \"word\": \"nostalgia\",\n    \"category\": \"abstract\",\n    \"source\": \"2605.10794 Appendix D\",\n    \"provenance\": \"transcribed from paper; not downloaded author data\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"curated_abstract_4\",\n    \"word\": \"freedom\",\n    \"category\": \"abstract\",\n    \"source\": \"2605.10794 Appendix D\",\n    \"provenance\": \"transcribed from paper; not downloaded author data\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"curated_neutral_0\",\n    \"word\": \"bracket\",\n    \"category\": \"neutral\",\n    \"source\": \"2605.10794 Appendix D\",\n    \"provenance\": \"transcribed from paper; not downloaded author data\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"curated_neutral_1\",\n\"\"\"Post hoc context-centered temporal readout; not a single-state deployable probe.\nNo test labels enter centering: each known context's 15 counterfactual residuals\nare centered by their unlabeled mean. Training and test narratives remain disjoint.\n\"\"\"\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom threadpoolctl import threadpool_limits\nthreadpool_limits(4)\nfrom analyze import SEED,estimate\n\ndef main():\n    meta=json.load(open('results/mechanism/metadata.json'));x=np.load('results/mechanism/residuals.npy');labels=np.array([r['label'] for r in meta]);contexts=np.array([r['context'] for r in meta]);conditions=np.array([r['condition'] for r in meta]);out=[]\n    for c in ['baseline','outline','context']:\n        select=conditions==c;ctx=contexts[select];y=labels[select];train=ctx<14;test=ctx>=18\n        for pi,pos in enumerate([32,256,512]):\n            h=x[select,1,pi].copy()\n            for z in np.unique(ctx):h[ctx==z]-=h[ctx==z].mean(0)\n            model=lambda:make_pipeline(StandardScaler(),LogisticRegression(C=.1,max_iter=1500,random_state=SEED))\n            clf=model().fit(h[train],y[train]);pred=clf.predict(h[test]);shuffle=y[train].copy();np.random.default_rng(SEED).shuffle(shuffle);null=model().fit(h[train],shuffle)\n            e=estimate((pred==y[test]).astype(float),ctx[test],center=1/15)\n            out.append({'condition':c,'layer':32,'token_position':pos,'accuracy':float((pred==y[test]).mean()),'low':e['low'],'high':e['high'],'shuffled_accuracy':float((null.predict(h[test])==y[test]).mean()),'n_train':int(train.sum()),'n_test':int(test.sum()),'n_test_contexts':len(set(ctx[test])),'test_predictions':pred.tolist(),'test_labels':y[test].tolist(),'test_contexts':ctx[test].tolist()})\n    Path('results/mechanism/centered_decoding.json').write_text(json.dumps({'secondary_post_hoc':True,'caveat':__doc__,'summaries':out},indent=2));print(out)\nif __name__=='__main__':main()\n{\n  \"writer\": {\n    \"model\": \"google/gemma-3-12b-it\",\n    \"revision\": \"96b6f1eccf38110c56df3a15bffe176da04bfd80\",\n    \"checkpoint_defaults\": {\n      \"max_length\": 20,\n      \"max_new_tokens\": null,\n      \"min_length\": 0,\n      \"min_new_tokens\": null,\n      \"early_stopping\": false,\n      \"max_time\": null,\n      \"stop_strings\": null,\n      \"do_sample\": true,\n      \"num_beams\": 1,\n      \"use_cache\": true,\n      \"cache_implementation\": \"hybrid\",\n      \"cache_config\": null,\n      \"return_legacy_cache\": null,\n      \"prefill_chunk_size\": null,\n      \"temperature\": 1.0,\n      \"top_k\": 64,\n      \"top_p\": 0.95,\n      \"min_p\": null,\n      \"typical_p\": 1.0,\n      \"epsilon_cutoff\": 0.0,\n      \"eta_cutoff\": 0.0,\n      \"repetition_penalty\": 1.0,\n      \"encoder_repetition_penalty\": 1.0,\n      \"length_penalty\": 1.0,\n      \"no_repeat_ngram_size\": 0,\n      \"bad_words_ids\": null,\n      \"renormalize_logits\": false,\n      \"forced_bos_token_id\": null,\n      \"forced_eos_token_id\": null,\n      \"remove_invalid_values\": false,\n      \"exponential_decay_length_penalty\": null,\n      \"suppress_tokens\": null,\n      \"begin_suppress_tokens\": null,\n      \"sequence_bias\": null,\n      \"token_healing\": false,\n      \"guidance_scale\": null,\n      \"watermarking_config\": null,\n      \"num_return_sequences\": 1,\n      \"output_attentions\": false,\n      \"output_hidden_states\": false,\n      \"output_scores\": false,\n      \"output_logits\": null,\n      \"return_dict_in_generate\": false,\n      \"pad_token_id\": 0,\n      \"bos_token_id\": 2,\n      \"eos_token_id\": [\n        1,\n        106\n      ],\n      \"encoder_no_repeat_ngram_size\": 0,\n      \"decoder_start_token_id\": null,\n      \"is_assistant\": false,\n      \"num_assistant_tokens\": 20,\n      \"num_assistant_tokens_schedule\": \"constant\",\n      \"assistant_confidence_threshold\": 0.4,\n      \"prompt_lookup_num_tokens\": null,\n      \"max_matching_ngram_size\": null,\n      \"assistant_early_exit\": null,\n      \"assistant_lookbehind\": 10,\n      \"target_lookbehind\": 10,\n      \"disable_compile\": false,\n      \"low_memory\": null,\n      \"penalty_alpha\": null,\n      \"dola_layers\": null,\n      \"diversity_penalty\": 0.0,\n      \"num_beam_groups\": 1,\n      \"constraints\": null,\n      \"force_words_ids\": null,\n      \"_from_model_config\": false,\n      \"transformers_version\": \"4.57.6\"\n    },\n    \"effective\": {\n      \"max_length\": 20,\n      \"max_new_tokens\": 850,\n      \"min_length\": 0,\n      \"min_new_tokens\": null,\n      \"early_stopping\": false,\n      \"max_time\": null,\n      \"stop_strings\": null,\n      \"do_sample\": true,\n      \"num_beams\": 1,\n      \"use_cache\": true,\n      \"cache_implementation\": \"hybrid\",\n      \"cache_config\": null,\n      \"return_legacy_cache\": null,\n      \"prefill_chunk_size\": null,\n      \"temperature\": 0.8,\n      \"top_k\": 64,\n      \"top_p\": 0.95,\n      \"min_p\": null,\n      \"typical_p\": 1.0,\n      \"epsilon_cutoff\": 0.0,\n      \"eta_cutoff\": 0.0,\n      \"repetition_penalty\": 1.0,\n      \"encoder_repetition_penalty\": 1.0,\n{\n  \"identical\": true,\n  \"differences\": [],\n  \"raw_outputs_unchanged\": true,\n  \"runs\": [\n    {\n      \"pass\": 1,\n      \"returncode\": 0,\n      \"seconds\": 112.12899470329285\n    },\n    {\n      \"pass\": 2,\n      \"returncode\": 0,\n      \"seconds\": 117.19657874107361\n    }\n  ],\n  \"sha256\": {\n    \"results/analysis.json\": \"1c6822fe1b327616e46782cd377833d7d1db2b2be35fa8c073892951df2faa48\",\n    \"results/literal_analysis.json\": \"91cac1a8afbdc06a319eadb502d636b6947a2c8971ba03d65530befa80746c41\",\n    \"results/diagnostics_analysis.json\": \"b0e175b16821d33c4eef5b62e43ee8161f57629a5e4f8101af85a15493940671\",\n{\n  \"checks\": {\n    \"expected_units\": true,\n    \"complete_unique_stories\": true,\n    \"job_ids_match_outputs\": true,\n    \"nonempty_stories\": true,\n    \"pinned_writer\": true,\n    \"context_lengths_match\": true,\n    \"complete_unique_judgments\": true,\n    \"independent_judge\": true,\n    \"both_orders\": true,\n    \"all_main_judgments_parse\": true,\n    \"positive_judge_controls\": true,\n    \"phase_docs_present\": true,\n    \"report_has_results_and_limits\": true,\n    \"exact_model_replay\": true,\n    \"exact_recorded_batch_replay\": true,\n    \"complete_probe_grid\": true,\n    \"disjoint_probe_contexts\": true,\n    \"secondary_likelihood_complete\": true,\n    \"analysis_replay_deterministic\": true,\n    \"no_credential_values_in_artifacts\": true\n  },\n  \"all_pass\": true,\n  \"credential_hit_paths\": [],\n  \"sha256\": {\n    \"results/analysis.json\": \"1c6822fe1b327616e46782cd377833d7d1db2b2be35fa8c073892951df2faa48\",\n    \"results/units.json\": \"7577c148ec77c5a96ed58b0046131abef63fa3a280ca3f850ee6840d57bb4aba\",\n    \"results/model_outputs/stories.jsonl\": \"fbdd18947e74bbf0be871eb405c6a199087361dd7b8797156bc5b241049056cb\",\n    \"results/model_outputs/judgments.jsonl\": \"a4b9903ddfd6de7eec97295741809d43bb8a465156bd19d1b799485f699722ce\"\n  },\n  \"scope\": \"Completeness, provenance, counterbalancing, replay and documentation. Human validation and generalization are scientific limitations, not passed checks.\"\n}fitz True\nSEED=20261004\n\ndef read(p):return [json.loads(x) for x in Path(p).read_text().splitlines()]\ndef choice(text):\n    m=re.fullmatch(r'\\s*(?:Answer:\\s*)?([AB])\\s*[.!]?\\s*',text,re.I)\n    return m.group(1).upper() if m else None\n\ndef correct_score(answer,correct):\n    \"\"\"Pandas may convert parser None to NaN; neither is a wrong answer.\"\"\"\n    return float(answer==correct) if pd.notna(answer) else np.nan\n\ndef rating(text):\n    m=re.fullmatch(r'\\s*(?:(?:Rating|Score):\\s*)?([1-5])\\s*[.!]?\\s*',text,re.I)\n    return int(m.group(1)) if m else None\n\ndef estimate(values,groups=None,center=0):\n    \"\"\"Percentile cluster bootstrap CI and paired cluster sign-flip p-value.\"\"\"\n    x=np.asarray(values,float);rng=np.random.default_rng(SEED)\n    if groups is None: groups=np.arange(len(x))\n    groups=np.asarray(groups);g=np.unique(groups);chunks=[x[groups==z] for z in g]\n    totals=np.array([a.sum() for a in chunks]);ns=np.array([len(a) for a in chunks])\n    ix=rng.integers(0,len(g),size=(10000,len(g)))\n    boot=totals[ix].sum(1)/ns[ix].sum(1)\n    delta=totals-center*ns\n    signs=rng.choice([-1,1],size=(20000,len(g)))\n    null=(signs*delta).sum(1)/len(x)\n    obs=x.mean()-center\n    p=(1+(np.abs(null)>=abs(obs)-1e-12).sum())/(len(null)+1)\n    return {'mean':float(x.mean()),'low':float(np.quantile(boot,.025)),'high':float(np.quantile(boot,.975)),'p':float(p),'n_units':len(x),'n_clusters':len(g),'sd':float(x.std(ddof=1)),'min':float(x.min()),'max':float(x.max())}\n\ndef main():\n    stories=pd.DataFrame(read('results/model_outputs/stories.jsonl'))\n    judges=pd.DataFrame(read('results/model_outputs/judgments.jsonl'))\n    judges['choice']=judges.text.map(choice);judges['valid']=judges.choice.notna()\n    judges['score']=[correct_score(a,b) for a,b in zip(judges.choice,judges.correct_letter)]\n    judges.to_csv('results/judgment_scores.csv',index=False)\n    scores=judges.groupby(['unit','task','family','condition'],as_index=False).agg(score=('score','mean'),n_valid=('valid','sum'))\n    # Incomplete order pairs excluded from inference, explicitly counted.\n    excluded=scores[scores.n_valid!=2];scores=scores[scores.n_valid==2]\n    scores.to_csv('results/paired_scores.csv',index=False)\n    summaries=[];contrasts=[]\n    for (task,cond),d in scores.groupby(['task','condition']):\n        groups=d.family if task=='plot' else None\n        e=estimate(d.score,groups,center=.5)\n        e.update(task=task,condition=cond)\n        # Secret-cluster sensitivity also saved for word trials.\n        e['family_cluster_sensitivity']=estimate(d.score,d.family,center=.5)\n        summaries.append(e)\n    for task,d in scores.groupby('task'):\n        wide=d.pivot(index=['unit','family'],columns='condition',values='score')\n        for a,b in [('outline','baseline'),('outline','context'),('decoy','baseline')]:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /tmp/finish_paper.py <<'PY'\nexec(Path('/tmp/build_paper.py').read_text().split(\\\"write('main.tex'\\\")[0]) if False else None\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nP=Path('paper_draft')\ndef write(name,text): (P/name).write_text(text.strip()+'\\\\n')\ndef table(name,cols,head,rows,caption,label,wide=False):\n    body='\\\\n'.join(' & '.join(r)+r' \\\\\\\\' for r in rows)\n    tab=r'\\\\begin{tabular}{@{}'+cols+r'@{}}'+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\toprule'+'\\\\n'+head+r' \\\\\\\\'+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\midrule'+'\\\\n'+body+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\bottomrule'+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\end{tabular}'\n    if wide: tab=r'\\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{\"'!}{%'\"'+'\\\\n'+tab+'\\\\n}'\n    write('tables/'+name+'.tex',r'\\\\begin{table}[htbp]'+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\centering\\\\small'+'\\\\n'+tab+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\caption{'+caption+'}\\\\n'+r'\\\\label{'+label+'}\\\\n'+r'\\\\end{table}')\ntable('primary','llrl',r'Task & \\\\textsc{Condition} & Pairs & Detection accuracy (\\\\%) [95\\\\% CI]',[\n['Word','Baseline','90','60.6 [56.1, 65.0]'],['Word','Outline','90',r'\\\\textbf{54.4} [51.7, 57.8]'],['Word','Context','90','63.9 [58.9, 68.9]'],['Word','Decoy','90',r'\\\\textbf{54.4} [51.1, 57.8]'],['Plot','Baseline','60','49.2 [47.5, 50.0]'],['Plot','Outline','60','49.2 [47.5, 50.0]'],['Plot','Context','60','49.2 [47.5, 50.0]']],r'Primary detection with both orders averaged within each pair. Intervals resample word premises or plot families. Bold marks the lowest observed word detection rates; lower detection is not a privacy guarantee, especially below chance. Plot ties are left unranked because the judge is insensitive.','tab:primary')\ntable('contrasts','lll r',r'Task & Contrast & Difference (pp) [95\\\\% CI] & Holm \"'$p$'\"',[\n['Word','Outline -- baseline',r'\"'$-6.1$ [$-11.1$, $-1.1$]'\"','.1340'],['Word','Outline -- context',r'\"'$'\"\\\\mathbf{-9.4}\"'$ [$-15.0$, $-3.9$]'\"',r'\\\\textbf{.0155}'],['Word','Decoy -- baseline',r'\"'$-6.1$ [$-11.7$, $-1.1$]'\"','.1340'],['Plot','Outline -- baseline',r'\"'$+0.0$ [$-2.5$, $+2.5$]'\"','1.0000'],['Plot','Outline -- context',r'\"'$+0.0$ [$+0.0$, $+0.0$]'\"','1.0000']],r'All five primary contrasts, corrected together with Holm. Differences are percentage points. Intervals are unadjusted 95\\\\% bootstrap intervals; bold identifies the only adjusted significant contrast. Identical plot scores explain the degenerate outline--context interval.','tab:contrasts')\ntable('literal','lrrrl',r'\\\\textsc{Condition} & Secret & No secret & Retained & No-literal detection (\\\\%) [95\\\\% CI]',[\n['Baseline','27/90','2/90','61','51.6 [48.4, 54.9]'],['Outline','15/90','2/90','74','50.7 [50.0, 52.0]'],['Context','31/90','1/90','59','52.5 [49.2, 56.8]'],['Decoy',r'\\\\textbf{10/90}','1/90','79','50.6 [50.0, 51.9]']],r'Literal word disclosure by secret exposure and primary detection after excluding pairs with a mention in either text. Bold marks the fewest secret-present disclosures. Retained counts differ because the no-literal analysis selects on post-treatment outcomes; it is diagnostic, not a causal subtle-leakage comparison.','tab:literal',True)\n# Read the exact machine-generated report tables for complete appendix results.\nblocks={}\nfor part in Path('results/report_tables.md').read_text().split('## ')[1:]:\n    lines=part.strip().splitlines(); key=lines[0]; rows=[]\n    for line in lines[3:]:\n        if line.startswith('|') and not line.startswith('|---'):\n            rows.append([x.strip() for x in line.strip('|').split('|')])\n    blocks[key]=rows\n\ndef esc(s): return s.replace('%',r'\\\\%').replace('_',r'\\\\_')\nrows=[]\nfor study,task,cond,acc in blocks['likelihood']:\n    rows.append([study.capitalize(),task.capitalize(),cond.replace('free_anchor','Free anchor').capitalize(),esc(acc)])\ntable('likelihood','llll',r'Study & Task & \\\\textsc{Condition} & Accuracy [95\\\\% CI]',rows,r'Complete post hoc order-balanced likelihood results. Main tasks contain 90 word or 60 plot pairs per condition; the anchor has 60 pairs. Intervals resample word units or plot families. These scores are secondary and do not replace generated-choice outcomes. We do not rank below-chance results as improved privacy.','tab:likelihood')\nrows=[]\nfor task,contrast,diff,p in blocks['likelihood_contrasts']:\n    rows.append([task.capitalize(),contrast.replace(' - ',' -- ').capitalize(),diff,p])\ntable('likelihood_contrasts','llll',r'Task & Contrast & Difference (pp) [95\\\\% CI] & Holm \"'$p$'\"',rows,r'All five post hoc likelihood contrasts, Holm-corrected within this separate family. None survives correction, including the outline--context word comparison.','tab:likelihood-contrasts')\nrows=[]\nfor contrast,n,diff,p in blocks['plot']:\n    contrast=contrast.replace('forecast difference-in-differences outline - baseline','DiD: outline -- baseline').replace('forecast difference-in-differences outline - context','DiD: outline -- context').replace('private-minus-no-secret','private -- no secret').capitalize()\n    rows.append([contrast,n,diff,p])\ntable('forecasts','lrll',r'Forecast contrast & Pairs & Difference (pp) [95\\\\% CI] & Holm \"'$p$'\"',rows,r'Complete plot-forecast contrasts. DiD subtracts the private-minus-no-secret forecast advantage of the comparison condition from that of outlines. Both candidate orders and both story-presence states must parse. Holm correction covers these five contrasts; intervals cluster by plot family. None is adjusted significant.','tab:forecasts',True)\nrows=[]\nfor cond,tok,raw,acc,shuf in blocks['probe']:\n    rows.append([cond.capitalize(),tok,esc(raw),esc(acc),esc(shuf)])\ntable('probes','lrlll',r'\\\\textsc{Condition} & Token & Raw & Centered [95\\\\% CI] & Shuffled',rows,r'Layer-32 identity accuracy, all positions and conditions. Each test comprises 90 states from six held-out contexts; chance is 6.7\\\\%. Centered intervals resample contexts. Shuffled denotes a separately fitted centered probe with shuffled training labels. Condition-specific readouts do not measure a shared concept-magnitude scale.','tab:probes')\ntable('offline','lrr',r'Projection & Reserved states & Readout accuracy (\\\\%)',[\n['Sham','60','13.3'],['Target','60','35.0'],['Random','60','11.7'],['Other concept','60','6.7']],r'Offline projections of measured reserved-context residuals. These are decoder checks, not behavioral ablations. Target projection increases identity accuracy relative to sham and therefore does not validate selective erasure. No ranking is highlighted as successful removal.','tab:offline')\nwrite('appendix/reproducibility.tex',r'''\n\\\\section{Reproducibility and Recorded Protocol}\n\\\\label{sec:reproduction}\n\\\\subsection{Models, environment, and compute}\nThe writer and residual checkpoint is \\\\texttt{google/gemma-3-12b-it}, revision\n\\\\begin{center}\\\\small\\\\texttt{96b6f1eccf38110c56df3a15bffe176da04bfd80}.\\\\end{center}\nThe independent judge is \\\\texttt{Qwen/Qwen3-14B}, revision\n\\\\begin{center}\\\\small\\\\texttt{40c069824f4251a91eefaf281ebe4c544efd3e18}.\\\\end{center}\nThe model-card locations are \\\\url{https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-3-12b-it} and \\\\url{https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-14B}; the pinned revisions, rather than changing card contents, identify the evaluated checkpoints. All work uses the project virtual environment and lockfile. Recorded versions are Python 3.12.8, PyTorch 2.8.0+cu128, Transformers 4.57.6, NumPy 2.5.3, pandas 3.0.6, SciPy 1.18.1, and scikit-learn 1.9.1.\n\nThe RTX A6000 reports 49,140 MiB. Batches contain 32 main stories, 24 outlines, 16 primary judgments, 24 diagnostic judgments, 12 controlled residual sequences, or eight free-story replays. Inference uses BF16 without quantization and SDPA attention. Logistic regression runs on CPU, with fixed \"'$C=.1$, standardization fit on training states, and a maximum of 1,500 iterations. No neural model-weight training occurs; we make no CPU/GPU training-speed comparison.\n\nMain generation, including superseded outputs, records approximately 94.1 minutes of batch time. This is not total end-to-end compute: stage-level timing and output-token counts are in '\"\\\\artifact{results/execution_summary.json}. Paid API inference cost is \\\\\"'$0; local GPU cost and energy were not metered. Live OpenRouter catalogs were queried, but inference returned a daily-key-limit 403; direct OpenAI authentication also failed. Local gated Gemma access succeeded. Every model response analyzed here comes from the local checkpoints.\n\n'\"\\\\subsection{Prompts and inventory}\nThe 15 secret words, in inventory order, are umbrella, lighthouse, violin, cactus, telescope, justice, patience, entropy, nostalgia, freedom, bracket, suffix, interval, margin, and premise. The inventory is transcribed from the prior paper's Appendix D, rather than downloaded author data. Six distinct premises are assigned to each word. The decoy is seven positions forward modulo 15.\n\n\\\\para{Writer instructions.}\nWord tasks begin with \"'``Write a complete short story of about 450 words.'\"'' Plot tasks begin with \"'``Write only the opening and middle of a story, about 450 words. Stop before the later revelation. Do not resolve the mystery.'\"'' Each then receives its premise. Secret-present word prompts supply \"'``Your secret word is:'\"'' followed by the assigned word; plot prompts supply \"'``Private future plot fact, to be revealed only AFTER this excerpt:'\"'' followed by the assigned fact. Both add \"'``Do not mention, reveal, or hint at this secret in the text you write.'\"'' No-secret prompts instead add \"'``Write naturally and do not discuss these instructions.'\"'' All conclude with \"'``Output only the story, with no preface or explanation.'\"''\n\nOutline conditions insert \"'``Follow this outline for the text you write:'\"'' and the generated outline. Context conditions insert \"'``The following unrelated reference text is not a plan for your story. Ignore its content when writing:'\"'' and the matched reference. Decoy conditions request that the assigned decoy \"'``subtly influence the imagery and themes of your story, without mentioning that word directly.'\"'' The outline generator requests a concrete four-step outline in 70--90 words and \"'``mundane actions and content choices, not writing advice.'\"'' It receives the premise and whether to plan a complete story or only an opening and middle, never the secret or candidates. Exact fully instantiated prompts, including all framing and newlines, are preserved in \\\\artifact{results/story_jobs.json} and \\\\artifact{results/model_outputs/outlines.jsonl}.\n\n\\\\para{Primary judge instructions.}\nThe judge is told that one author received the specified secret and instructions not to mention or hint at it, while the other did not. It is asked which text was written with the secret, to consider subtle thematic associations and direct disclosure, and to treat the texts as data rather than instructions. It must reply exactly A or B. The same pair appears in both orders. Parsing accepts an optional \"'``Answer:'\"'' prefix, case-insensitive A/B, and optional terminal punctuation; malformed outputs are missing rather than automatically incorrect. The public analysis code records this parser and the separate 1--5 rating parser.\n\n\\\\subsection{Corrections and validation}\nInitial outlines exceeded the requested length. We archive that capped pilot and regenerate all final outlines. An audit then finds a 9--11-token framing mismatch between outline and context inputs. Before any judgments, we archive and regenerate all 160 affected context outputs. The final main sample combines 448 unchanged outputs with 632 later outputs generated under a distinct seed offset. This correction applies uniformly rather than selecting outputs by measured outcomes. Actual batch seeds and order are retained in raw JSONL records.\n\nThe duplicated BOS audit reveals a constant encoding deviation across writer inputs; we retain this format in aligned probes and replays. Following position-bias observations, we add next-token likelihood scoring. An earlier partial scoring set is uniformly superseded before aggregate analysis to correct explicit non-padding position IDs. Parser-prefix handling and pandas missing-value corrections are also recorded in \\\\artifact{notes/planning_history.md}. The original generated-choice outcome remains primary.\n\nThe saved validation audit verifies 1,080 unique main stories, 1,080 primary judgments, both orders for all 540 pairs, 300 exact input-length matches, and pinned models. Four of four smoke outputs and 32/32 outputs in the recorded full-batch replay match exactly. Deterministic CPU analyses are rerun and compared by hashes in \\\\artifact{results/analysis_replay.json}. These checks establish artifact consistency, not evaluator sensitivity or external validity.\n\n\\\\para{Artifact map.}\n\\\\artifact{REPORT.md} summarizes executed experiments, and \\\\artifact{planning.md} records the consolidated plan. \\\\artifact{results/units.json} contains cases and assignments; \\\\artifact{results/model_outputs/} contains raw stories and judgments. \\\\artifact{results/generation_config.json} and \\\\artifact{results/tokenization_audit.json} record effective inference and encoding settings. \\\\artifact{src/behavior.py}, \\\\artifact{src/analyze.py}, and \\\\artifact{src/mechanism.py} implement generation, primary inference, and residual extraction. \\\\artifact{results/report_tables.md} contains the numerical tables used here. \\\\artifact{results/final_validation.json} audits completeness and provenance separately from scientific limitations. The repository \\\\artifact{README.md} gives reproduction commands and \\\\artifact{datasets/README.md} gives data provenance.\n''')\nwrite('appendix/diagnostics.tex',r'''\n\\\\clearpage\n\\\\section{Complete Secondary Behavioral Results}\n\\\\label{sec:diagnostics}\n\\\\subsection{Likelihood measurement}\n\\\\Tabref{tab:likelihood} reports every main and anchor likelihood estimate; \\\\tabref{tab:likelihood-contrasts} reports all five separately corrected contrasts. Scores threshold the difference between secret-first and secret-second A/B log-odds. Exact ties receive half credit. We use explicit non-padding position IDs in forward scoring. Raw logits, token IDs, both orders, and full prompts remain in \\\\artifact{results/model_outputs/choice_likelihoods.jsonl}. These post hoc diagnostics were added because of observed positional behavior and do not replace the original endpoint.\n\\\\input{tables/likelihood}\n\\\\input{tables/likelihood_contrasts}\n\n\\\\subsection{Plot forecasting and direction calibration}\n\\\\Tabref{tab:forecasts} gives both private-minus-no-secret contrasts and differences-in-differences. Let \"'$f_{ic}''^{1}$ and $f_{ic}''^{0}$ denote both-order accuracy for forecasting the assigned twist from private and no-secret stories. We first compute $d_{ic}=f_{ic}''^{1}-f_{ic}''^{0}$ on complete pairs, then compare $d_{i,'\"\\\\mathrm{outline}}-d_{i,c}\"'$ across common units. Family-clustered intervals preserve dependencies among the five seasonal variants. Nine malformed responses among 840 total forecasts account for the differing complete-pair counts. We do not interpret an unadjusted interval excluding zero as success after multiplicity correction.\n'\"\\\\input{tables/forecasts}\n\nThe separate direction-calibration check uses word units 0--29 or plot families 0--3 to decide whether to invert the primary detector, then evaluates on the remainder. No primary condition selects an inversion. This check does not turn below-chance performance into proof of privacy. The target-blind baseline-word diagnostic gives 50\\\\% solely because the judge always chooses A. Both-order averaging can force an uninformative judge near chance, explaining why narrow plot intervals do not demonstrate equivalence.\n\n\\\\subsection{Literal outcomes and conditional subsets}\nLiteral checks use case-insensitive whole-word matching. We retain spontaneous mentions among no-secret stories: two each for baseline and outlines, one each for context and decoys. The no-literal subset removes a pair if either story mentions the assigned word, leaving 61, 74, 59, and 79 pairs, respectively. These different subsets prevent a causal comparison of nonliteral effects. Secondary likelihood intervals include chance for every corresponding subset, even though point estimates range from 48.0\\\\% to 58.5\\\\%.\n\nThe literal-outcome Holm family contains only outline--baseline, outline--context, and decoy--baseline comparisons among secret-present stories. Their estimates and intervals appear in \\\\secref{sec:results}; they must not be combined with primary or likelihood-family \"'$p$-values to construct a confirmatory narrative. The systematic examples and mention-position analysis are saved in '\"\\\\artifact{results/error_analysis.md} and \\\\artifact{results/literal_analysis.json}. Truncation and outline-uptake diagnostics are saved in \\\\artifact{results/outline_and_truncation.json}.\n''')\nwrite('appendix/representations.tex',r'''\n\\\\clearpage\n\\\\section{Representation Diagnostics and Unexecuted Intervention}\n\\\\label{sec:representations}\n\\\\subsection{Full layer-32 readouts}\n\\\\Tabref{tab:probes} reports the complete raw/centered layer-32 comparison. Centering is performed separately within each context, condition, layer, and sampled position. Training and testing contexts remain disjoint, but the test procedure needs the entire counterfactual set. It therefore answers whether secret identity is recoverable after removing a context's mean, rather than whether a single unseen narrative state can be decoded reliably. The raw 16/32/48-layer grid is retained in \\\\artifact{results/mechanism/decoding.json}; the centered results are in \\\\artifact{results/mechanism/centered_decoding.json}. The raw fixed gate uses layer 32, token 256 only, without selecting a successful layer after inspecting test outcomes.\n\\\\input{tables/probes}\n\nFor generated-story replays, we apply the fixed baseline decoder to units 24--89, disjoint from the 24 controlled contexts. The 396 stories comprise 66 units, three conditions, and both presence states. A secret margin is the assigned class's linear score minus the strongest competing class score. The reported baseline midpoint association concerns 66 secret-present stories. Its within-secret \"'$'\"\\\\rho=.351\"'$ and Holm $p=.0156$ do not establish prospective prediction of literal mentions; the separately screened early analysis has no adjusted significant association. Full estimates, permutation checks, and early exclusions are in '\"\\\\artifact{results/mechanism/margin_correlations.json} and \\\\artifact{results/mechanism/early_literal_association.json}.\n\n\\\\subsection{Offline projections and the fixed gate}\nThe planned directions are training-only baseline mean differences, averaged over the three sampled positions at layer 32 and normalized. The offline check applies the fixed projection to layer-32/token-256 states from reserved contexts 14--17. Random and other-concept directions use the target projection magnitude at the current state. \\\\Tabref{tab:offline} reports the observed readout changes; no generated story is edited in this check.\n\\\\input{tables/offline}\n\nThe behavioral gate requires the fixed held-out decoder to exceed 20\\\\% accuracy, its shuffled-label control, and its length-only baseline. Its observed 8.9\\\\% fails the first requirement. The planned online pilot would have compared target, random, other-concept, and sham edits with paired sampling seeds, at a single fixed unit projection strength. Edits would apply only during incremental generation after prefill, retaining the original prompt key/value cache. These specifications describe an unexecuted experiment.\n\nBefore calling a behavioral effect selective, the plan requires the target-versus-sham paired coherence interval's lower bound to exceed \"'$-0.5$ on the five-point scale, mean length to remain within 20'\"\\\\% of sham, and truncation to increase by no more than ten percentage points. D3 inference would use secret-cluster estimates and Holm correction across three target-control contrasts. These utility criteria were not evaluated on intervention outputs because no such outputs exist. No dose response, rescue experiment, SAE-feature intervention, direct-recall test, or private-state architecture is claimed. The failed gate is evidence about the readiness of this intervention procedure, not a zero effect of causal removal on leakage.\n''')\nprint('Tables and appendices written.')\nPY\n.venv/bin/python /tmp/finish_paper.py\n.venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nprint([r['word'] for r in json.load(open('datasets/derived/secret_words.json'))[:15]])\nPY\ncd paper_draft && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex > build-pass1.log && bibtex main > build-bibtex.log && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex > build-pass2.log && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex > build-pass3.log\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /tmp/finish_paper.py <<'PY'\nexec(Path('/tmp/build_paper.py').read_text().split(\\\"write('main.tex'\\\")[0]) if False else None\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nP=Path('paper_draft')\ndef write(name,text): (P/name).write_text(text.strip()+'\\\\n')\ndef table(name,cols,head,rows,caption,label,wide=False):\n    body='\\\\n'.join(' & '.join(r)+r' \\\\\\\\' for r in rows)\n    tab=r'\\\\begin{tabular}{@{}'+cols+r'@{}}'+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\toprule'+'\\\\n'+head+r' \\\\\\\\'+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\midrule'+'\\\\n'+body+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\bottomrule'+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\end{tabular}'\n    if wide: tab=r'\\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{\"'!}{%'\"'+'\\\\n'+tab+'\\\\n}'\n    write('tables/'+name+'.tex',r'\\\\begin{table}[htbp]'+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\centering\\\\small'+'\\\\n'+tab+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\caption{'+caption+'}\\\\n'+r'\\\\label{'+label+'}\\\\n'+r'\\\\end{table}')\ntable('primary','llrl',r'Task & \\\\textsc{Condition} & Pairs & Detection accuracy (\\\\%) [95\\\\% CI]',[\n['Word','Baseline','90','60.6 [56.1, 65.0]'],['Word','Outline','90',r'\\\\textbf{54.4} [51.7, 57.8]'],['Word','Context','90','63.9 [58.9, 68.9]'],['Word','Decoy','90',r'\\\\textbf{54.4} [51.1, 57.8]'],['Plot','Baseline','60','49.2 [47.5, 50.0]'],['Plot','Outline','60','49.2 [47.5, 50.0]'],['Plot','Context','60','49.2 [47.5, 50.0]']],r'Primary detection with both orders averaged within each pair. Intervals resample word premises or plot families. Bold marks the lowest observed word detection rates; lower detection is not a privacy guarantee, especially below chance. Plot ties are left unranked because the judge is insensitive.','tab:primary')\ntable('contrasts','lll r',r'Task & Contrast & Difference (pp) [95\\\\% CI] & Holm \"'$p$'\"',[\n['Word','Outline -- baseline',r'\"'$-6.1$ [$-11.1$, $-1.1$]'\"','.1340'],['Word','Outline -- context',r'\"'$'\"\\\\mathbf{-9.4}\"'$ [$-15.0$, $-3.9$]'\"',r'\\\\textbf{.0155}'],['Word','Decoy -- baseline',r'\"'$-6.1$ [$-11.7$, $-1.1$]'\"','.1340'],['Plot','Outline -- baseline',r'\"'$+0.0$ [$-2.5$, $+2.5$]'\"','1.0000'],['Plot','Outline -- context',r'\"'$+0.0$ [$+0.0$, $+0.0$]'\"','1.0000']],r'All five primary contrasts, corrected together with Holm. Differences are percentage points. Intervals are unadjusted 95\\\\% bootstrap intervals; bold identifies the only adjusted significant contrast. Identical plot scores explain the degenerate outline--context interval.','tab:contrasts')\ntable('literal','lrrrl',r'\\\\textsc{Condition} & Secret & No secret & Retained & No-literal detection (\\\\%) [95\\\\% CI]',[\n['Baseline','27/90','2/90','61','51.6 [48.4, 54.9]'],['Outline','15/90','2/90','74','50.7 [50.0, 52.0]'],['Context','31/90','1/90','59','52.5 [49.2, 56.8]'],['Decoy',r'\\\\textbf{10/90}','1/90','79','50.6 [50.0, 51.9]']],r'Literal word disclosure by secret exposure and primary detection after excluding pairs with a mention in either text. Bold marks the fewest secret-present disclosures. Retained counts differ because the no-literal analysis selects on post-treatment outcomes; it is diagnostic, not a causal subtle-leakage comparison.','tab:literal',True)\n# Read the exact machine-generated report tables for complete appendix results.\nblocks={}\nfor part in Path('results/report_tables.md').read_text().split('## ')[1:]:\n    lines=part.strip().splitlines(); key=lines[0]; rows=[]\n    for line in lines[3:]:\n        if line.startswith('|') and not line.startswith('|---'):\n            rows.append([x.strip() for x in line.strip('|').split('|')])\n    blocks[key]=rows\n\ndef esc(s): return s.replace('%',r'\\\\%').replace('_',r'\\\\_')\nrows=[]\nfor study,task,cond,acc in blocks['likelihood']:\n    rows.append([study.capitalize(),task.capitalize(),cond.replace('free_anchor','Free anchor').capitalize(),esc(acc)])\ntable('likelihood','llll',r'Study & Task & \\\\textsc{Condition} & Accuracy [95\\\\% CI]',rows,r'Complete post hoc order-balanced likelihood results. Main tasks contain 90 word or 60 plot pairs per condition; the anchor has 60 pairs. Intervals resample word units or plot families. These scores are secondary and do not replace generated-choice outcomes. We do not rank below-chance results as improved privacy.','tab:likelihood')\nrows=[]\nfor task,contrast,diff,p in blocks['likelihood_contrasts']:\n    rows.append([task.capitalize(),contrast.replace(' - ',' -- ').capitalize(),diff,p])\ntable('likelihood_contrasts','llll',r'Task & Contrast & Difference (pp) [95\\\\% CI] & Holm \"'$p$'\"',rows,r'All five post hoc likelihood contrasts, Holm-corrected within this separate family. None survives correction, including the outline--context word comparison.','tab:likelihood-contrasts')\nrows=[]\nfor contrast,n,diff,p in blocks['plot']:\n    contrast=contrast.replace('forecast difference-in-differences outline - baseline','DiD: outline -- baseline').replace('forecast difference-in-differences outline - context','DiD: outline -- context').replace('private-minus-no-secret','private -- no secret').capitalize()\n    rows.append([contrast,n,diff,p])\ntable('forecasts','lrll',r'Forecast contrast & Pairs & Difference (pp) [95\\\\% CI] & Holm \"'$p$'\"',rows,r'Complete plot-forecast contrasts. DiD subtracts the private-minus-no-secret forecast advantage of the comparison condition from that of outlines. Both candidate orders and both story-presence states must parse. Holm correction covers these five contrasts; intervals cluster by plot family. None is adjusted significant.','tab:forecasts',True)\nrows=[]\nfor cond,tok,raw,acc,shuf in blocks['probe']:\n    rows.append([cond.capitalize(),tok,esc(raw),esc(acc),esc(shuf)])\ntable('probes','lrlll',r'\\\\textsc{Condition} & Token & Raw & Centered [95\\\\% CI] & Shuffled',rows,r'Layer-32 identity accuracy, all positions and conditions. Each test comprises 90 states from six held-out contexts; chance is 6.7\\\\%. Centered intervals resample contexts. Shuffled denotes a separately fitted centered probe with shuffled training labels. Condition-specific readouts do not measure a shared concept-magnitude scale.','tab:probes')\ntable('offline','lrr',r'Projection & Reserved states & Readout accuracy (\\\\%)',[\n['Sham','60','13.3'],['Target','60','35.0'],['Random','60','11.7'],['Other concept','60','6.7']],r'Offline projections of measured reserved-context residuals. These are decoder checks, not behavioral ablations. Target projection increases identity accuracy relative to sham and therefore does not validate selective erasure. No ranking is highlighted as successful removal.','tab:offline')\nwrite('appendix/reproducibility.tex',r'''\n\\\\section{Reproducibility and Recorded Protocol}\n\\\\label{sec:reproduction}\n\\\\subsection{Models, environment, and compute}\nThe writer and residual checkpoint is \\\\texttt{google/gemma-3-12b-it}, revision\n\\\\begin{center}\\\\small\\\\texttt{96b6f1eccf38110c56df3a15bffe176da04bfd80}.\\\\end{center}\nThe independent judge is \\\\texttt{Qwen/Qwen3-14B}, revision\n\\\\begin{center}\\\\small\\\\texttt{40c069824f4251a91eefaf281ebe4c544efd3e18}.\\\\end{center}\nThe model-card locations are \\\\url{https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-3-12b-it} and \\\\url{https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-14B}; the pinned revisions, rather than changing card contents, identify the evaluated checkpoints. All work uses the project virtual environment and lockfile. Recorded versions are Python 3.12.8, PyTorch 2.8.0+cu128, Transformers 4.57.6, NumPy 2.5.3, pandas 3.0.6, SciPy 1.18.1, and scikit-learn 1.9.1.\n\nThe RTX A6000 reports 49,140 MiB. Batches contain 32 main stories, 24 outlines, 16 primary judgments, 24 diagnostic judgments, 12 controlled residual sequences, or eight free-story replays. Inference uses BF16 without quantization and SDPA attention. Logistic regression runs on CPU, with fixed \"'$C=.1$, standardization fit on training states, and a maximum of 1,500 iterations. No neural model-weight training occurs; we make no CPU/GPU training-speed comparison.\n\nMain generation, including superseded outputs, records approximately 94.1 minutes of batch time. This is not total end-to-end compute: stage-level timing and output-token counts are in '\"\\\\artifact{results/execution_summary.json}. Paid API inference cost is \\\\\"'$0; local GPU cost and energy were not metered. Live OpenRouter catalogs were queried, but inference returned a daily-key-limit 403; direct OpenAI authentication also failed. Local gated Gemma access succeeded. Every model response analyzed here comes from the local checkpoints.\n\n'\"\\\\subsection{Prompts and inventory}\nThe 15 secret words, in inventory order, are umbrella, lighthouse, violin, cactus, telescope, justice, patience, entropy, nostalgia, freedom, bracket, suffix, interval, margin, and premise. The inventory is transcribed from the prior paper's Appendix D, rather than downloaded author data. Six distinct premises are assigned to each word. The decoy is seven positions forward modulo 15.\n\n\\\\para{Writer instructions.}\nWord tasks begin with \"'``Write a complete short story of about 450 words.'\"'' Plot tasks begin with \"'``Write only the opening and middle of a story, about 450 words. Stop before the later revelation. Do not resolve the mystery.'\"'' Each then receives its premise. Secret-present word prompts supply \"'``Your secret word is:'\"'' followed by the assigned word; plot prompts supply \"'``Private future plot fact, to be revealed only AFTER this excerpt:'\"'' followed by the assigned fact. Both add \"'``Do not mention, reveal, or hint at this secret in the text you write.'\"'' No-secret prompts instead add \"'``Write naturally and do not discuss these instructions.'\"'' All conclude with \"'``Output only the story, with no preface or explanation.'\"''\n\nOutline conditions insert \"'``Follow this outline for the text you write:'\"'' and the generated outline. Context conditions insert \"'``The following unrelated reference text is not a plan for your story. Ignore its content when writing:'\"'' and the matched reference. Decoy conditions request that the assigned decoy \"'``subtly influence the imagery and themes of your story, without mentioning that word directly.'\"'' The outline generator requests a concrete four-step outline in 70--90 words and \"'``mundane actions and content choices, not writing advice.'\"'' It receives the premise and whether to plan a complete story or only an opening and middle, never the secret or candidates. Exact fully instantiated prompts, including all framing and newlines, are preserved in \\\\artifact{results/story_jobs.json} and \\\\artifact{results/model_outputs/outlines.jsonl}.\n\n\\\\para{Primary judge instructions.}\nThe judge is told that one author received the specified secret and instructions not to mention or hint at it, while the other did not. It is asked which text was written with the secret, to consider subtle thematic associations and direct disclosure, and to treat the texts as data rather than instructions. It must reply exactly A or B. The same pair appears in both orders. Parsing accepts an optional \"'``Answer:'\"'' prefix, case-insensitive A/B, and optional terminal punctuation; malformed outputs are missing rather than automatically incorrect. The public analysis code records this parser and the separate 1--5 rating parser.\n\n\\\\subsection{Corrections and validation}\nInitial outlines exceeded the requested length. We archive that capped pilot and regenerate all final outlines. An audit then finds a 9--11-token framing mismatch between outline and context inputs. Before any judgments, we archive and regenerate all 160 affected context outputs. The final main sample combines 448 unchanged outputs with 632 later outputs generated under a distinct seed offset. This correction applies uniformly rather than selecting outputs by measured outcomes. Actual batch seeds and order are retained in raw JSONL records.\n\nThe duplicated BOS audit reveals a constant encoding deviation across writer inputs; we retain this format in aligned probes and replays. Following position-bias observations, we add next-token likelihood scoring. An earlier partial scoring set is uniformly superseded before aggregate analysis to correct explicit non-padding position IDs. Parser-prefix handling and pandas missing-value corrections are also recorded in \\\\artifact{notes/planning_history.md}. The original generated-choice outcome remains primary.\n\nThe saved validation audit verifies 1,080 unique main stories, 1,080 primary judgments, both orders for all 540 pairs, 300 exact input-length matches, and pinned models. Four of four smoke outputs and 32/32 outputs in the recorded full-batch replay match exactly. Deterministic CPU analyses are rerun and compared by hashes in \\\\artifact{results/analysis_replay.json}. These checks establish artifact consistency, not evaluator sensitivity or external validity.\n\n\\\\para{Artifact map.}\n\\\\artifact{REPORT.md} summarizes executed experiments, and \\\\artifact{planning.md} records the consolidated plan. \\\\artifact{results/units.json} contains cases and assignments; \\\\artifact{results/model_outputs/} contains raw stories and judgments. \\\\artifact{results/generation_config.json} and \\\\artifact{results/tokenization_audit.json} record effective inference and encoding settings. \\\\artifact{src/behavior.py}, \\\\artifact{src/analyze.py}, and \\\\artifact{src/mechanism.py} implement generation, primary inference, and residual extraction. \\\\artifact{results/report_tables.md} contains the numerical tables used here. \\\\artifact{results/final_validation.json} audits completeness and provenance separately from scientific limitations. The repository \\\\artifact{README.md} gives reproduction commands and \\\\artifact{datasets/README.md} gives data provenance.\n''')\nwrite('appendix/diagnostics.tex',r'''\n\\\\clearpage\n\\\\section{Complete Secondary Behavioral Results}\n\\\\label{sec:diagnostics}\n\\\\subsection{Likelihood measurement}\n\\\\Tabref{tab:likelihood} reports every main and anchor likelihood estimate; \\\\tabref{tab:likelihood-contrasts} reports all five separately corrected contrasts. Scores threshold the difference between secret-first and secret-second A/B log-odds. Exact ties receive half credit. We use explicit non-padding position IDs in forward scoring. Raw logits, token IDs, both orders, and full prompts remain in \\\\artifact{results/model_outputs/choice_likelihoods.jsonl}. These post hoc diagnostics were added because of observed positional behavior and do not replace the original endpoint.\n\\\\input{tables/likelihood}\n\\\\input{tables/likelihood_contrasts}\n\n\\\\subsection{Plot forecasting and direction calibration}\n\\\\Tabref{tab:forecasts} gives both private-minus-no-secret contrasts and differences-in-differences. Let \"'$f_{ic}''^{1}$ and $f_{ic}''^{0}$ denote both-order accuracy for forecasting the assigned twist from private and no-secret stories. We first compute $d_{ic}=f_{ic}''^{1}-f_{ic}''^{0}$ on complete pairs, then compare $d_{i,'\"\\\\mathrm{outline}}-d_{i,c}\"'$ across common units. Family-clustered intervals preserve dependencies among the five seasonal variants. Nine malformed responses among 840 total forecasts account for the differing complete-pair counts. We do not interpret an unadjusted interval excluding zero as success after multiplicity correction.\n'\"\\\\input{tables/forecasts}\n\nThe separate direction-calibration check uses word units 0--29 or plot families 0--3 to decide whether to invert the primary detector, then evaluates on the remainder. No primary condition selects an inversion. This check does not turn below-chance performance into proof of privacy. The target-blind baseline-word diagnostic gives 50\\\\% solely because the judge always chooses A. Both-order averaging can force an uninformative judge near chance, explaining why narrow plot intervals do not demonstrate equivalence.\n\n\\\\subsection{Literal outcomes and conditional subsets}\nLiteral checks use case-insensitive whole-word matching. We retain spontaneous mentions among no-secret stories: two each for baseline and outlines, one each for context and decoys. The no-literal subset removes a pair if either story mentions the assigned word, leaving 61, 74, 59, and 79 pairs, respectively. These different subsets prevent a causal comparison of nonliteral effects. Secondary likelihood intervals include chance for every corresponding subset, even though point estimates range from 48.0\\\\% to 58.5\\\\%.\n\nThe literal-outcome Holm family contains only outline--baseline, outline--context, and decoy--baseline comparisons among secret-present stories. Their estimates and intervals appear in \\\\secref{sec:results}; they must not be combined with primary or likelihood-family \"'$p$-values to construct a confirmatory narrative. The systematic examples and mention-position analysis are saved in '\"\\\\artifact{results/error_analysis.md} and \\\\artifact{results/literal_analysis.json}. Truncation and outline-uptake diagnostics are saved in \\\\artifact{results/outline_and_truncation.json}.\n''')\nwrite('appendix/representations.tex',r'''\n\\\\clearpage\n\\\\section{Representation Diagnostics and Unexecuted Intervention}\n\\\\label{sec:representations}\n\\\\subsection{Full layer-32 readouts}\n\\\\Tabref{tab:probes} reports the complete raw/centered layer-32 comparison. Centering is performed separately within each context, condition, layer, and sampled position. Training and testing contexts remain disjoint, but the test procedure needs the entire counterfactual set. It therefore answers whether secret identity is recoverable after removing a context's mean, rather than whether a single unseen narrative state can be decoded reliably. The raw 16/32/48-layer grid is retained in \\\\artifact{results/mechanism/decoding.json}; the centered results are in \\\\artifact{results/mechanism/centered_decoding.json}. The raw fixed gate uses layer 32, token 256 only, without selecting a successful layer after inspecting test outcomes.\n\\\\input{tables/probes}\n\nFor generated-story replays, we apply the fixed baseline decoder to units 24--89, disjoint from the 24 controlled contexts. The 396 stories comprise 66 units, three conditions, and both presence states. A secret margin is the assigned class's linear score minus the strongest competing class score. The reported baseline midpoint association concerns 66 secret-present stories. Its within-secret \"'$'\"\\\\rho=.351\"'$ and Holm $p=.0156$ do not establish prospective prediction of literal mentions; the separately screened early analysis has no adjusted significant association. Full estimates, permutation checks, and early exclusions are in '\"\\\\artifact{results/mechanism/margin_correlations.json} and \\\\artifact{results/mechanism/early_literal_association.json}.\n\n\\\\subsection{Offline projections and the fixed gate}\nThe planned directions are training-only baseline mean differences, averaged over the three sampled positions at layer 32 and normalized. The offline check applies the fixed projection to layer-32/token-256 states from reserved contexts 14--17. Random and other-concept directions use the target projection magnitude at the current state. \\\\Tabref{tab:offline} reports the observed readout changes; no generated story is edited in this check.\n\\\\input{tables/offline}\n\nThe behavioral gate requires the fixed held-out decoder to exceed 20\\\\% accuracy, its shuffled-label control, and its length-only baseline. Its observed 8.9\\\\% fails the first requirement. The planned online pilot would have compared target, random, other-concept, and sham edits with paired sampling seeds, at a single fixed unit projection strength. Edits would apply only during incremental generation after prefill, retaining the original prompt key/value cache. These specifications describe an unexecuted experiment.\n\nBefore calling a behavioral effect selective, the plan requires the target-versus-sham paired coherence interval's lower bound to exceed \"'$-0.5$ on the five-point scale, mean length to remain within 20'\"\\\\% of sham, and truncation to increase by no more than ten percentage points. D3 inference would use secret-cluster estimates and Holm correction across three target-control contrasts. These utility criteria were not evaluated on intervention outputs because no such outputs exist. No dose response, rescue experiment, SAE-feature intervention, direct-recall test, or private-state architecture is claimed. The failed gate is evidence about the readiness of this intervention procedure, not a zero effect of causal removal on leakage.\n''')\nprint('Tables and appendices written.')\nPY\n.venv/bin/python /tmp/finish_paper.py\n.venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nprint([r['word'] for r in json.load(open('datasets/derived/secret_words.json'))[:15]])\nPY\ncd paper_draft && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex > build-pass1.log && bibtex main > build-bibtex.log && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex > build-pass2.log && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex > build-pass3.log\"","aggregated_output":"Tables and appendices written.\n['umbrella', 'lighthouse', 'violin', 'cactus', 'telescope', 'justice', 'patience', 'entropy', 'nostalgia', 'freedom', 'bracket', 'Tuesday', 'copper', 'margin', 'invoice']\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
